{"created": "2026-07-29T13:02:52.289930+00:00", "modified": "2026-07-29T13:02:52.741484+00:00", "id": 21675842, "conceptrecid": "21675841", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21675842", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.21675841", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21675842", "metadata": {"title": "From Policy to Proof: How Aurora-Lens Can Strengthen Runtime Evidence within an ISO/IEC 42001 Artificial Intelligence Management System", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21675842", "publication_date": "2026-07-29", "description": "<p class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><em>From Policy to Proof</em> examines how Aurora-Lens can provide decision-level runtime evidence within an ISO/IEC 42001 artificial intelligence management system (AIMS).</p>\n<p>ISO/IEC 42001 establishes an organisational framework for AI policy, responsibility, risk management, operational control, monitoring, audit and continual improvement, while remaining technology-neutral about how controls are implemented. This paper addresses the resulting evidentiary question: when model output approaches a consequential boundary, can an organisation show what its governance control actually did in that particular instance?</p>\n<p>Aurora-Lens is presented as one possible runtime evidence layer. Positioned between an application and a model provider, it evaluates candidate output before release, supports governed non-commitment outcomes, preserves governing state across interactions, distinguishes informative records from still-valid authority, and generates tamper-evident decision records. The paper distinguishes management-system evidence from decision-level evidence, maps Aurora-Lens contributions to selected ISO/IEC 42001 requirement areas and Annex A control families, and identifies the limits of runtime records, deterministic evaluation and cryptographic integrity.</p>\n<p>Aurora-Lens does not independently establish ISO/IEC 42001 conformity, certify an organisation, prove the truth of recorded premises or replace organisational accountability. Its narrower contribution is to strengthen the implementation and auditability of selected controls by recording what was proposed, which governing state and policy applied, what outcome was produced, and what content was released or withheld at the point of consequence.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Stokes, Margaret", "affiliation": null, "orcid": "0009-0004-6422-4174"}], "keywords": ["ISO/IEC 42001", "ISO 42001", "AI management system", "AIMS", "AI Governance", "runtime governance", "admissibility", "decision-level evidence", "audit evidence", "tamper-evident ledger", "governed non-commitment", "AI assurance", "conformity assessment", "Aurora-Lens"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.19504665", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.21123912", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.21397474", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.19879302", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}], "resource_type": {"title": "Preprint", "type": "publication", "subtype": "preprint"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "21675841"}}]}}, "title": "From Policy to Proof: How Aurora-Lens Can Strengthen Runtime Evidence within an ISO/IEC 42001 Artificial Intelligence Management System", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21675842", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21675842", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21675842?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21675842", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21675842", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.21675842", "reserve_doi": "http